A. Securing REST APIs requires a multi-layered approach including strong authentication, authorization, encryption, input validation, and continuous monitoring throughout the API lifecycle.
Authentication and Authorization
Every API request should be authenticated to verify the caller and authorized to ensure they have permission for the requested action. 
Use OAuth 2.0, JWTs, or API keys combined with role-based (RBAC) or attribute-based access control (ABAC) to enforce fine-grained permissions. 
Avoid long-lived tokens and implement short token lifespans. For sensitive endpoints, consider multi-factor authentication (MFA) to reduce privilege escalation risks 

Input Validation and Output Filtering
Treat all incoming data as untrusted. Validate inputs using schemas, whitelists, type checks, and length limits to prevent SQL/NoSQL injection, unsafe deserialization, and other injection attacks.
Filter outputs to expose only necessary fields,
ideally using data transfer objects (DTOs) or response schemas to prevent excessive data exposure 

Encryption
Use HTTPS/TLS 1.2 or higher (preferably TLS 1.3) for all traffic, including internal service-to-service communication. 
Encrypt sensitive fields in payloads and consider AES-256 for data at rest. Disable outdated protocols like SSLv3 and TLS 1.0 to prevent interception or tampering 

Rate Limiting and Throttling
Implement rate limits to prevent abuse, credential stuffing, and denial-of-service attacks. Introduce progressive backoff or CAPTCHAs after repeated failed authentication attempts to mitigate brute-force attacks 

Secure API Lifecycle
Integrate security from design to deployment. Conduct threat modeling, secure coding practices, automated CI/CD testing, and continuous monitoring. 
Perform regular security audits, penetration testing, and runtime protection to detect and block anomalies or malicious activity 

Additional Measures
Shadow endpoint discovery: Identify and secure undocumented endpoints to prevent unauthorized access 
Token validation: Validate tokens on each request to prevent misuse 
Server-side request validation: Sanitize URLs and hostnames to reduce SSRF risks 
Principle of least privilege: Limit access to only what is necessary for each user or service 

